Designing an ‘Inventory Management and Supply Chain Network’ software for the DPE 
Context: Primary education system in Bangladesh is one of the largest education management system in the world. 
It consists about 66000 government run primary schools, 64 district primary education offices, 67 primary teachers 
training institutes, 8 divisional offices, 514 Upzailla education offices and 505 URCs that include about 2,05,00,000 
students and 5 lakhs teaching staff. No doubt, DPE’s has to deal with huge number of regular goods, electronic goods 
and accessories and other regular usable things by addressing people’s demand. It has to deal with numerous goods 
procurement and supply to its incumbents. This is near too hard to manage supply goods and manage inventory 
manually with full potential. There is an obvious chance to misuse goods and that the government money. However, 
in response to the growing demands and supply matter the government needs to rethink the issue seriously to manage 
it properly in the meaning of utilizing goods properly, reducing misuse, and preserve it through using an automation 
system such as developing an improved software to maintain the quality of supply and inventory management. This 
provision could address the issue of good governance and institutional strengthening including assuring the customer 
satisfaction.                    
Concept Note: Development of an Automated Supply and Inventory Management Software for the Primary 
Education System in Bangladesh 
1. Introduction: 
The primary education system in Bangladesh is one of the largest educational management endeavor in the world, 
comprising about 66,000 government-run primary schools, 64 district primary education offices, 67 primary teachers training 
institutes, 8 divisional offices, 514 upazila education offices, and 505 upazilla resource centers, catering to approximately 
2,05,00,000 students and employing 5 lakhs teaching and management staff. The enormous scale of operations 
necessitates a robust supply and inventory management system to ensure efficient handling of goods, wealth, and 
resources to recognize good governance and create a strong organization.   
2. Problem Statement: 
The existing manual system for managing the supply, inventory of goods and align with procurement system in the 
Directorate of Primary Education (DPE) is challenging to handle given the extensive network of offices including schools. 
Manual processes are prone to errors, misuse, and inefficiencies, leading to potential financial losses and compromised 
service delivery which is crucial to develop. In response to these challenges, there is a critical need for an automated 
solution that streamlines the supply chain and enhances inventory management of the DPE.  
 
3. Objective: 
The primary objective of this initiative is to develop an improved and customized software solution that automates the supply 
and inventory management processes within the primary education system. The software aims to improve the efficiency, 
transparency, and accountability of the potential distribution of goods, ultimately ensuring the judicious utilization of 
resources and reducing the risk of misuse which could minimize the financial losses and improve the quality of service 
delivery.  
4. Scope: 
The proposed software will cover the entire supply chain, including warehouse and storage management, distribution, and 
monitoring of goods. It will integrate with existing systems and databases to ensure seamless data flow and real-time 
tracking. The scope also includes user training and ongoing technical support to facilitate a smooth transition to the new 
system. It may create a positive scope to establish an extensive network to the supplier and end users including build a 
network with other stakeholders.    
5. Key Features: 
• Align with the Automated Procurement: Implement a system for automated procurement based on demand 
forecasts and historical usage data to prevent overstocking or understocking of goods. 
• Inventory Tracking: Develop a robust inventory tracking system with real-time updates on stock levels, 
expirations, needs, and usage patterns. 
• User Access Control: Implement role-based access controls to ensure that only authorized personnel can initiate, 
approve, and monitor distribution and procurement activities. 
• Integration: Integrate the software with existing databases and systems to facilitate seamless data exchange and 
avoid duplication of efforts. 
• Reporting and Analytics: Provide comprehensive reporting tools and analytics for informed decision-making, 
budget planning, and resource optimization including provide recommendations.  
6. Expected Outcomes: 
• Efficiency Improvement: Streamline supply chain processes, reducing manual workload, increasing the efficiency 
of goods distribution and warehouse management. 
• Cost Reduction: Minimize the risk of financial losses due to misuse or mismanagement of goods, leading to cost 
savings for the government. 
• Transparency and Accountability: Enhance transparency in the supply chain, making it easier to track 
transactions and hold responsible parties accountable. 
• Customer Satisfaction: Improve the overall quality of service delivery by ensuring a timely and reliable supply of 
goods to offices and schools. 
• Permanent Documentation: Ensure a permanent documentation of resources and wealth through managing 
data-base which leads to a solution of assets management.  
7. Implementation Plan: 
• Phase 1: Needs Assessment and Planning (2 months): Identify specific requirements, conduct a thorough 
needs assessment workshop, and develop a detailed project plan. 
• Phase 2: Software Development (3 months): Collaborate with development partner(s) to build and customize the 
software according to the identified needs and specifications. 
• Phase 3: Testing and Refinement (2 months): Conduct rigorous testing to identify and address any bugs or 
issues, refining the software for optimal performance by piloting the project.  
• Phase 4: Training and Implementation (3 months): Provide training to end-users, such as field-level officials and 
implement the software in a phased manner across the primary education system. 
• Phase 5: Recommendation for further development  
8. Budget and Resources: 
To be prepared a detailed budget encompassing development costs, training expenses, and ongoing support discussing 
with the development partner. Allocation of required human resources for project management, software development, 
training and implementation.   
9. Monitoring and Evaluation: 
To establish a monitoring and evaluation framework in tracking the performance of the software post-implementation, gather 
user feedback, and make necessary adjustments for continuous improvement.  
10. Conclusion: 
The development of an automated supply and inventory management software for the primary education system in 
Bangladesh is a critical step towards achieving efficient, transparent, and accountable governance. By partnering with a 
reliable development agency (s), we can ensure the successful implementation of this priceless initiative, positively 
impacting the overall quality of education services and resource management in the country by using an improved software.  
 
 